`aplan check-docs` answers one question: does this document name tasks that
exist? An item reading "handled in `Delta`" when no such task exists reads as
*handled*, which is worse than an item with no disposition at all (`A1-148`).

Ids come from the plan rather than from a pattern. The prefixes this plan's own
ids use are what a word must carry to be read as a reference, so the same command
works on `A1-042`, `WH2-06`, `K0-3` and `M-phase-2` without this tool owning an
id format. `M-v0.N`, which is how prose writes "a milestone", is not a reference
to `M-v0`: a reference ends at a word boundary as well as starting at one.

The plan also teaches whether a numeric family continues through hyphenated
suffixes. The P00024-shaped ids below therefore resolve as complete references,
including across backticks and ordinary punctuation.

```
$ aplan check-docs MULTI.md
ok: 1 document(s), 0 reference(s) naming no task

```

An unknown member of that family is likewise reported whole. Truncating this to
`M-p0` would send the reader looking for a task the document never named.

```
$ aplan check-docs MULTI-DANGLING.md
? 1
MULTI-DANGLING.md:3: `M-p0-review` is not a task in this plan
failed: 1 document(s), 1 reference(s) naming no task

```

```
$ aplan check-docs NOTES.md
? 1
NOTES.md:5: `D1-09` is not a task in this plan (did you mean D1-01, D1-02?)
NOTES.md:10: `D1-07` is not a task in this plan (did you mean D1-01, D1-02?)
NOTES.md:10: `D1-08` is not a task in this plan (did you mean D1-01, D1-02?)
NOTES.md:13: `D2-01` is not a task in this plan, whose ids use `D1-` (did you mean D1-01?)
failed: 1 document(s), 4 reference(s) naming no task

```

The same, machine-readable, for a gate that reports rather than fails:

```
$ aplan check-docs NOTES.md --format json
? 1
{
  "documents": 1,
  "findings": [
    {
      "did_you_mean": [
        "D1-01",
        "D1-02"
      ],
      "file": "NOTES.md",
      "id": "D1-09",
      "line": 5,
      "mistyped_prefix": []
    },
    {
      "did_you_mean": [
        "D1-01",
        "D1-02"
      ],
      "file": "NOTES.md",
      "id": "D1-07",
      "line": 10,
      "mistyped_prefix": []
    },
    {
      "did_you_mean": [
        "D1-01",
        "D1-02"
      ],
      "file": "NOTES.md",
      "id": "D1-08",
      "line": 10,
      "mistyped_prefix": []
    },
    {
      "did_you_mean": [
        "D1-01"
      ],
      "file": "NOTES.md",
      "id": "D2-01",
      "line": 13,
      "mistyped_prefix": [
        "D1-"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "membership": [],
  "schema": "aplan/check-docs/v1"
}

```

A plan document listing a cut's tasks beside its milestone holds two copies of one
fact, and until something reads both, one of them is ungated. `docs.membership`
in `.aplan.yml` says where the document declares it, and the check is silent for
a repository that declares nothing.

Only the marked line declares. A mention in the same section is prose, which is
the distinction the whole check rests on: reading a cut's section instead of its
declaration calls an ordinary forward reference a false declaration. So is a
fenced example, even one that is a marked line verbatim: the document below shows
its own format inside a fence, naming both tasks, and the finding stands.

```
$ aplan check-docs PLAN.md
? 1
PLAN.md: `M-v0.1` includes `D1-02`, which this document does not declare
failed: 1 document(s), 0 reference(s) naming no task, 1 cut(s) whose membership disagrees

```

The other shape a plan document takes: a task table per cut, the member in the
row's first cell (ADR-0015) and prose in the rest. `M-v0.1` sits in a second cell
here and is not read as a member, which is why this run is clean.

```
$ aplan check-docs TABLE.md
ok: 1 document(s), 0 reference(s) naming no task

```

The same, machine-readable. A membership row carries both directions and the two
findings that are about the declaration itself rather than about its contents.

```
$ aplan check-docs PLAN.md --format json
? 1
{
  "documents": 1,
  "findings": [],
  "membership": [
    {
      "file": "PLAN.md",
      "milestone": "M-v0.1",
      "no_declaration": false,
      "repeated_section": false,
      "undeclared": [
        "D1-02"
      ],
      "unplanned": []
    }
  ],
  "schema": "aplan/check-docs/v1"
}

```

Two headings naming one cut is the author's ambiguity, and the gate says so rather
than picking one. Taking the first is how a document whose *second* section
disagreed with the plan used to pass.

```
$ aplan check-docs DUPLICATE.md
? 1
DUPLICATE.md: more than one heading here names `M-v0.1`, so which one declares its membership is ambiguous
failed: 1 document(s), 0 reference(s) naming no task, 1 cut(s) whose membership disagrees

```

A document the config does not name is checked for references alone: the two runs
over `NOTES.md` above are made under this same config and report no cut, so a run
over one file never reports the membership of another.

The diagnostic formats belong to the rules, and a document naming a task is not a
plan violating an invariant, so it carries no `APLAN0xx` code and those formats
are refused rather than collapsed to JSON.

```
$ aplan check-docs NOTES.md --format github
? 2
aplan: `aplan check-docs` renders a report, so it takes --format `human` or `json`, not `github`; `github` renders a set of findings, which `check` and `verify` do

```

A plan whose ids teach no prefix -- words, or the ULIDs `id_strategy: ulid` mints
-- gives this nothing to recognise, and every document would come back clean. A
gate that cannot fire says so instead: reporting `ok` here would be the same
"reads as handled" failure, arriving from the tool.

```
$ aplan --root wordy check-docs wordy/NOTES.md
? 2
aplan: this plan's ids teach no prefix (an id with a numeric core, like `T-042` or `M-p0-design`, teaches its family), so nothing in a document can be told from an ordinary word; `check-docs` checked nothing

```
